.TH MESG 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
mesg \- control write access to your terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mesg
.RB [ y | n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mesg
reports or changes whether other users are permitted to send messages
(for example, with
.BR write (1))
to the invoking terminal, by way of the terminal device's own
group\-write permission bit. The terminal affected is found by
checking, in order, standard input, standard output, and standard
error, for the first descriptor that is really a terminal.
.PP
With no operand, the current state is reported without being changed:
"\fBis y\fR"
if messages are currently allowed,
"\fBis n\fR"
if not.
.B y
allows messages, by setting the group\-write bit on the terminal's
device node.
.B n
denies them, by clearing it.
.SH REAL BEHAVIOR BY PLATFORM
On the native\-Linux backend, the affected terminal is a real character
device with a real device\-node path, and its permission bit is a
real, checkable, settable filesystem permission:
.BR y / n
here do exactly what
.BR mesg (1)
has traditionally done, and genuinely gate whether a different process
can open that device for writing.
.PP
On the Windows backend, a console is correctly recognized as a
terminal, but has no filesystem permission bit this implementation can
read or change at all: nothing here ever actually blocks another
process from writing to it. Consistent with that reality, bare
.B mesg
and
.B "mesg y"
succeed, honestly reporting (or confirming) the only state that has
ever really applied \(em messages are never blocked \(em while
.B "mesg n"
is refused outright, with a diagnostic and a nonzero exit status,
rather than being accepted and then silently not honored.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.B y
Allow other users to send messages to this terminal.
.TP
.B n
Deny other users permission to send messages to this terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Receiving messages is allowed.
.TP
.B 1
Receiving messages is not allowed. This is
.BR "mesg n" 's
own successful exit status, not a failure.
.TP
.B >1
An error occurred: invalid arguments, no terminal could be found among
standard input/output/error, a failure reading or changing the
terminal's permission bit, or
.B "mesg n"
was requested on a terminal for which this implementation has no
mechanism to actually deny messages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR write (1),
.BR tty (1),
.BR stty (1)
